  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Border of Water and Ice is already showing early scholarly and teaching reach. The book has 2 OpenAlex citations, 674 downloads, and appears in two recorded university and scholarly video discussions, suggesting that it is being read, cited, and used in academic settings soon after publication.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Joseph A. Seeley\u2019s Border of Water and Ice examines the Yalu River as a border shaped by seasonal ice, flooding, and the movement of people, goods, and state power during Japanese imperial expansion in Korea and Manchuria. The book\u2019s scholarly footprint is still modest, but the record shows genuine early uptake: it has been cited twice, with one citation in 2025 and another in 2026, indicating that other researchers are beginning to build from its arguments.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation trail also suggests disciplinary relevance beyond a single niche. The citing works include an article in Environmental History and a reference entry in Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Asian History, pointing to interest from historians and scholars working at the intersection of environment, borderlands, and East Asian studies. The citing landscape is concentrated in U.S.-based educational institutions, which is consistent with an emerging academic readership rather than broad public circulation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data reinforce that reading demand is active. The book has recorded 674 downloads across its available open-access channels, with most of the measurable activity coming through OAPEN and Ubiquity Press. That pattern, together with its hybrid open-access availability, indicates that the book is accessible to readers beyond a single platform and is finding an audience soon after release.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There are also signals of teaching and professional discussion. Two recorded YouTube events feature the book in university and scholarly contexts, including a distinguished speaker series at Lingnan University and a Modern Japan History Association new-books discussion. In addition, LibraryThing identifies the book as a finalist for the ACLS Open Access Book Prize and Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award in history in 2026, adding a further marker of recognition in the open-access scholarly ecosystem.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, Border of Water and Ice presents a focused but credible impact profile for a recent scholarly book: early citation uptake, measurable download activity, and visible use in academic discussions. The evidence points to growing scholarly attention rather than mature field-wide diffusion, and the record will be most informative as citations and teaching references continue to accumulate.</p>",
